# VIGIL SaaS findings-crossing drain — config for vigil-finding-drain.service
# Copy to ~/.config/vigil/finding-drain.env and fill in.
# =====================================================================
# IMPORTANT — paths in THIS file must be ABSOLUTE. systemd does NOT expand %h (or any %-specifier) inside an
# EnvironmentFile value; a "%h/..." here reaches the drain as the LITERAL string and the CLI refuses it (the
# drain exits non-zero → a FAILED dead-man alarm) rather than silently draining the wrong directory. The unit
# itself ships working %h-expanded DEFAULTS for the spool and scope (in vigil-finding-drain.service's
# Environment= directives, where %h DOES expand), so the only thing you must set below is the delegation.

# REQUIRED: the owner-signed OFFENSE_GOVERNANCE delegation JSON that authorizes FINDINGS. Provision this per
# deployment (it is the owner tie that makes a crossed finding trustworthy); the drain is fail-closed without
# it. Use an ABSOLUTE path (replace /home/USER with this unit's home).
VIGIL_FINDING_GOV_DELEGATION=/home/USER/.config/vigil/offense-governance-delegation.json

# OPTIONAL OVERRIDES (each defaults from the .service; override only with an ABSOLUTE path):
#   VIGIL_FINDING_SPOOL   the finding spool whose incoming/ is drained (default %h/.sigil/spool/findings)
#   VIGIL_FINDING_SCOPE   confine ingest to one engagement ("*" = any the delegation covers; or a cust_<hash> slug)
# VIGIL_FINDING_SPOOL=/home/USER/.sigil/spool/findings
# VIGIL_FINDING_SCOPE=*

# OPTIONAL: to also cross DETECTION FACTs, provision an OFFENSE_SPINE delegation and add
#   --spine-delegation ${VIGIL_FINDING_SPINE_DELEGATION}
# to the unit's ExecStart, then set (ABSOLUTE path):
# VIGIL_FINDING_SPINE_DELEGATION=/home/USER/.config/vigil/offense-spine-delegation.json

# OPTIONAL: set SIGIL_HOME if the sovereign spine home is not the default (then add it to the unit's
# ReadWritePaths). ABSOLUTE path.
# SIGIL_HOME=/home/USER/.sigil

# NOTE: the dead-man ENABLE flag does NOT belong here. VIGIL_FINDING_DRAIN_ENABLED is read by the vigil-alerts
# MONITOR process, not by this drain — set it in the monitor's env (~/.config/vigil/alerts.env), never in this
# file (where nothing reads it). It is only an EARLY-ARM: once the drain has fired once, its heartbeat arms the
# dead-man automatically. See vigil-alerts.env.example.
